# Euclid's GCD: a recursive algorithm with integer modulo, expressed in Glass and
# Default proofs are sound but not zero-knowledge: add --zk to hide the private inputs.
# lowered to a zero-knowledge circuit. The base case `b == 0` makes the recursive arm
# `gcd(b, a%b)` DEAD at the last step, so its `a % 0` is built but never selected: it
# proves only because of dead-branch predication (the modulo gadget's r<b
# check is vacuous when the divisor is 0). Composes recursion + `%` + the predicated
# divide-by-zero, all in one classic algorithm.
#
#   glass prove --cross-check examples/prove/gcd_prove.glass x=48 y=18    # gcd(48,18) = 6
#
# (Inlining is capture-avoiding. A sequential let-binding of the arguments would capture the `a` in
#  `gcd(b, a % b)`, so the circuit would attest 18 (the cross-check flags that divergence). With
#  capture-avoiding inlining this header holds end-to-end: 6, ACCEPT, three lineages agree.)
#
# NOTE: the proof circuit is large: recursion unrolls to the fuel bound (8 levels),
# each carrying a full modulo gadget (~hundreds of range-decomposition gates), so the
# native STARK is heavy (tens of minutes of CPU: fuel-8 unroll = 8 full divmod gadgets). The reference interpreter confirms the semantics
# instantly (`--cross-check` / the suite's cross-check gate); a faster prover (the
# lookup-range argument on the roadmap) is what makes deep recursive divmod practical.

fn gcd(a: Int, b: Int) : Int = if b == 0 then a else gcd(b, a % b)

gcd(x, y)
